// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-09-05 (Saturday): Xenharmonic tuning experiment — 31edo
// ============================================================================
//
// THE TUNING: 31edo divides the octave into 31 EQUAL steps of 1200/31 ≈
// 38.71 cents each. Historically real, not a novelty division: Christiaan
// Huygens described its close fit to quarter-comma meantone in the 17th
// century, and physicist Adriaan Fokker built a working 31-tone organ off
// the back of it in the 20th. Checked against the prior two Saturdays
// before writing a note of this (daily/2026-08-22-xenharmonic-17edo.strudel,
// daily/2026-08-29-xenharmonic-19edo.strudel) so today's arrangement is a
// fresh decision, not last cycle's template with new pitch numbers — see
// ARRANGEMENT below for what's actually different this week.
//
// WHY 31edo, SPECIFICALLY: like 19edo it's a meantone system (5 large steps
// + 2 small steps per octave = 31, same generator logic as 19edo's meantone
// diatonic scale), but it goes further — its major third (10 steps ≈
// 387.1c) sits within ~1 cent of the pure 5:4 ratio (386.3c), AND its
// harmonic/septimal seventh (25 steps ≈ 967.7c) sits within ~1 cent of the
// pure 7:4 ratio (968.8c). 12edo's minor seventh (1000c) misses 7:4 by over
// 30 cents and can never lock in tune the way this can. That's the specific,
// citable "wow" of 31edo: it's accurate enough to stack a genuine 4:5:6:7
// harmonic-seventh chord — root, major third, fifth, harmonic seventh — and
// hear it ring nearly beatless, a chord 12edo can only ever approximate.
//
// THE CHORD (raw 31edo steps, xen() convention: step 0 = 220Hz, octave = 31
// steps, repeats at 2/1):
//   root  =  0   (unison)
//   third = 10   (~387c, ~1c from just 5:4)
//   fifth = 18   (~697c, standard 31edo/meantone fifth)
//   7th   = 25   (~968c, ~1c from just 7:4 — the "septimal" seventh)
//   8ve   = 31
//
// ARRANGEMENT, deliberately not last two Saturdays': 17edo used a separate
// gm_pad_warm drone + acid-distorted sawtooth bass (edgy neutral third suited
// acid's tension); 19edo dropped the acid bass for a Sound-on-Sound
// kalimba-through-delay drone (a sweet meantone third suited consonance, not
// edge). Neither fits today: the point this week isn't one melodic third,
// it's a whole 4-note chord locking into near-just tuning, so the drone
// becomes an actual SUSTAINED CHORD VOICING — four separate sine tones (one
// per chord tone above), each panned to its own position across the stereo
// field and held for the full clip, so the ear can hear all four intervals
// ring together rather than one drone note at a time. Also, per
// MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §2.5 (synth basics on theory-driven days, not gm_*
// acoustic samples): both prior Saturdays used gm_kalimba for melody/drone;
// today drops that entirely in favor of sine (chord pad, for the purest tone
// so the beating/non-beating is easy to hear) and supersaw (lead, for a
// harmonically dense "plucked" attack per §2.5's own suggestion) — the first
// Saturday to apply that correction. The lead ARPEGGIATES the same four
// chord tones instead of running a euclidean scale-wander (both priors put
// `.euclid()` directly on the melody) — today's euclidean rhythm moves to
// the percussion layer instead, sized 4 against 31 (four pulses, tied to the
// chord's own 4-note cardinality, not the usual 7-note-scale count reused
// both prior weeks) so the melody can stay a clean, fully-voiced arpeggio.
//
// MELODY (four alternating phrases, degrees against the chord tones above
// plus two passing tones — 5 = whole-tone neighbor, 41 = third an octave up):
//   A (call, rising arpeggio across the whole chord):     0 ~ 10 18 ~ 25 31
//   B (response, falls back through the chord, resolves):  25 18 ~ 10 ~ 0
//   C (retrograde of A — literal reverse, QC-loop device): 31 25 ~ 18 10 ~ 0
//   D (peak: Goa-trance octave pitch-lift on the third,
//      MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §6, using only pitches already established):
//                                              0 10 18 25 ~ 41 ~ 25 18 0
//   rotation <A B C D> over the 15-cycle clip.
//
// CYCLES: 15. setcpm(45) -> 15 * 60/45 = 20s.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(45)
let CYCLES = 15

stack(
  // sustained 4:5:6:7 chord, one voice per interval, spread across the
  // stereo field and held for the entire clip — the arrangement's whole
  // point: let all four near-just intervals ring together and be heard
  // locking in tune, not passed through one at a time
  stack(
    i("-31").xen("31edo").sound("sine").pan(0.15), // root, one octave down
    i("-21").xen("31edo").sound("sine").pan(0.38), // third
    i("-13").xen("31edo").sound("sine").pan(0.62), // fifth
    i("-6").xen("31edo").sound("sine").pan(0.85),  // harmonic 7th
  ).slow(CYCLES).attack(3).release(5).gain(.24).room(3),

  // lead: arpeggiates the chord itself rather than wandering the scale —
  // supersaw for a harmonically dense "plucked" attack (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md
  // §2.5), no euclidean gaps here so the full chord always reads clearly
  i("<[0 ~ 10 18 ~ 25 31] [25 18 ~ 10 ~ 0] [31 25 ~ 18 10 ~ 0] [0 10 18 25 ~ 41 ~ 25 18 0]>")
    .xen("31edo")
    .sound("supersaw")
    .attack(.01).decay(.18).sustain(.1).release(.12)
    .lpf(2200)
    .room(1.5)
    .gain(.4)
    .sometimes(x => x.ply(2)), // improv hook: occasional double-strike

  // bass: plain sine sub, parked on the tonic almost the whole time with an
  // occasional visit to the fifth (Goa-trance bass convention, §6) — two
  // octaves down, nothing fighting the chord's ring
  i("<-62 -62 -62 -44>/4")
    .xen("31edo")
    .sound("sine")
    .lpf(500)
    .gain(.3)
    .attack(.02).release(.4),

  // percussion: euclid(4,31) ties the rhythm to the chord's own 4-note
  // count (root-third-fifth-seventh), not the usual 7-note-scale derivation
  // both prior Saturdays used — a fresh rhythmic idea for a fresh topic
  s("rim(4,31)").gain(.15).room(2),
  s("hh*8").degradeBy(.5).gain(.08),
)
